tests {
  test("returns 409 Conflict", function () {
    // The drawer disables the button, but a client that ignores `alreadyLogged`
    // — a replayed request, a stale page, curl — must not get a second lead.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(409);
  });

  test("refuses without naming the agency that owns it", function () {
    const message = JSON.stringify(res.getBody());
    expect(message).to.not.contain("demo");
    expect(message).to.not.contain("{{campaignMailerLeadId}}");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /mailers/log-lead — a second agency is refused (PAC-71)

  The other half of the cross-agency contract. **Log Campaign Mailer Lead** gave
  the demo agency this mailer; this is another tenant trying to take it.

  - **Module:** `mailers` + `leads`
  - **Permission:** `mailers:read` + `leads:write`

  ## Why 409 and not a silent second lead

  PAC-71 open item 7, resolved with David: **one lead per mailer, platform-wide.**
  The first agency to log it owns it. Two agencies each holding "their own" lead
  for one mailed household would double-count the campaign in every report built
  on `Lead.mailer.campaignId`, and the two producers would call the same person.

  The refusal is enforced in two places, not one: `logLead` pre-checks and
  returns this 409, and a **unique partial index** `{ 'mailer.mailerId': 1 }`
  catches the race the pre-check cannot — an E11000 on it is mapped to the same
  409. A check alone would be a time-of-check/time-of-use bug under two
  concurrent drawers.

  ⚠ The message deliberately does not name the owning agency, the producer or
  the lead. A tenant learning *who else* is working a household from an error
  body would be a cross-tenant leak through the one path that is supposed to be
  a refusal.

  ## Errors
  - `409 Conflict` — this mailer already produced a lead, in some agency.
  - `404 Not Found` — the mailer is not visible to the caller's agency at all
    (an `agencies`-mode campaign that did not include them). Note the asymmetry:
    invisible is a 404, visible-but-taken is a 409, and only the second tells
    the caller the mailer exists — which they could already see from the lookup.
}
